.\" Manual page for mosdns-cdnctl, the control tool this package installs.
.TH MOSDNS\-CDNCTL 1 "2026-09-27" "mosdns-router 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mosdns\-cdnctl \- render, measure, prove and publish this router's configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I render
.RB [ \-\-policy\ \fIPATH\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-out\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I validate
.B \-\-policy
.I PATH
.RB [ \-\-documents\ \fIDIR\fR ]
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I update\-lists
.RB ( \-\-check | \-\-pin\-remote\ \fIHEAD\fR | \-\-refresh\-ranges )
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I test
.RB [ \-\-apply ]
.RB [ \-\-report\ \fIPATH\fR ]
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I apply
.I REPORT
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I health\-check
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I status
.RB ( \-\-selector\ \fIPATH\fR | \-\-dhcp\ \fIPATH\fR | \-\-ech\ \fIPATH\fR )
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I pin
.I ADDRESS
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I unpin
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I check\-upstream
.RB ( \-\-all | \fINAME\fR )
.RB [ \-\-policy\ \fIPATH\fR ]
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I flush\-cache
.br
.B mosdns\-cdnctl
.I emergency\-rollback
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mosdns\-cdnctl (1)
is this project's control tool. It is what turns a policy into the two documents
the gateway runs, what measures and publishes the CDN address in service, and what
proves that address is still good. Three of its five jobs are started by timers
rather than by a person, and one of them is root\-only by design.
.SS render
Loads a policy, renders all three generated files through the same renderers this
repository reviewed, and publishes them: the two documents as a pair \(em the
routing document first and the resolver document second, each staged, flushed and
renamed into place, with the previous one kept until the pair is complete \(em and
then the systemd timer, staged and renamed the same way. A policy that cannot be
loaded or cannot be validated is reported as one error and nothing is published.
.PP
The timer is published into
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/mosdns-list\-check.timer ,
because that is where systemd reads it, and it is not published beside the
documents: a timer in
.I /etc/mosdns
would be a file no reader looks at. Its
.B OnCalendar
is generated from the policy's
.BR schedule ,
so this command is how a changed schedule takes effect, and
.B systemctl daemon\-reload
runs as part of writing it rather than as a step you have to remember. If the
reload fails the command says so and still exits zero, because everything on disk
is then correct and a non\-zero exit would send you looking for a render failure
that did not happen.
.PP
.B \-\-unit\-out
names the directory the timer is published into, and render creates it. It is a
separate flag from
.B \-\-out
because the two files go to different places, and one flag meaning \"the output
directory\" for two directories is how a machine ends up with a policy that says
one hour and a timer that fires at another.
.PP
The policy path it is given is the path both published documents NAME, so rendering
a policy somewhere other than
.I /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
produces documents that refer to that other path. That is what the
.B \-\-policy
and
.B \-\-out
flags are for, and it is why this package builds its own copy through a private
view of the filesystem rather than substituting a path into the result.
.SS validate
Reports whether the installed documents are what the policy describes, and writes
nothing. The policy itself is valid \(em that is what the caller established \(em
so the command still exits zero; what it adds is the disagreement between a policy
nobody applied and a document the router is running. A directory with no document
in it is an installation that has not rendered yet and is not reported as stale.
.SS update\-lists
Four mutually exclusive modes, and each is a mode rather than a flag on the
others: they write different things, and a flag that changed that would be a flag
whose name understated it.
.TP
.B \-\-automatic
The mode the scheduled run uses, and the one
.BR mosdns\-list\-check.service (8)
executes. It always produces the drift report \em that report is the only place
this machine's answer about its own source is written down, so a machine with
unattended refreshing turned on must not thereby stop being told \em and then
refreshes exactly what
.I /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
says to refresh, which by default is nothing at all. With both switches off it does
precisely what
.B \-\-check
did, so turning the feature on and off changes the configuration rather than the
machine. It is not a modifier on
.BR \-\-check ,
so it cannot be combined with any of the other three.
.br
The two switches are separate because the risks are not the same kind.
.B lists.cloudflare.automatic
refreshes which CDN edge an answer is rewritten to;
.B lists.china.automatic
decides which names take the foreign branch at all. Bundling them would mean
opening the cheap one opens the expensive one. Both are off in the shipped policy:
.B data/cn
is curated by hand, so any upstream commit moves the split, and accepting one
unattended is a routing change nobody reviewed.
.br
The China refresh refuses a list that has lost more than 5% of its rules, and says
both counts when it does. The digest cannot catch this, because a commit whose
list is a fraction of its former size verifies perfectly \em it really is the
upstream document. The refusal leaves the pin, the list and the rollback archive
exactly as they were, so publishing that commit afterwards is one deliberate
command an operator runs having read the diff. The threshold is not a refusal to
ever update: a curated list does lose domains now and then.
.br
Everything that can fail happens before anything is written, so a machine with no
route out fails without having touched the pin, the list or the archive.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Reports whether the pinned source has moved, and reports the published ranges read
from disk without requesting them. Takes no lock, writes nothing, and cannot
publish a missing prefix list. Scheduled daily by
.BR mosdns\-list\-check.timer (5).
.br
It also reports the range snapshot this package ships: its two digests, the
moment it was taken, how old it is, and whether the document published on this
machine is still the one the snapshot holds. That is the only measurement of how
stale the shipped pin is, and a three\-month\-old pin is a perfectly good input
for a selector while a pin nobody measures is not. The report costs no request,
so it is printed on the failure path as well \em on standard error, with standard
output left empty, because a machine with no route to the internet is exactly the
machine whose China\-list request fails.
.TP
.B \-\-pin\-remote\ \fIHEAD\fR
.br
.B \-\-pin\-remote\ \fICOMMIT\fR
Re\-pins the China domain list and publishes the list and the lock that describes
it. With
.B HEAD
\(the default, and the documented invocation) it takes the source's current
default\-branch commit. With a full 40\-character commit it publishes that commit
instead, which is how a pin accepted by mistake is undone: the rollback has to be
able to name where it is going back to, and \"whatever upstream says now\" is not
an answer to that question. A branch, a tag, an abbreviated commit and
.B HEAD~1
are all refused at the command line rather than after a request \em the lock
records a commit, and none of those is one.
.br
Before publishing, the outgoing lock is archived to
.I /var/lib/mosdns/lists/source-lock.previous.json
and is then replaced by the new one. That order is the mechanism rather than a
detail: the moment the new lock is renamed into place the old one exists nowhere,
so an archive taken afterwards would record the commit that was just accepted
instead of the one this machine was on. Taken before, a publication that fails
leaves an archive naming a commit that really was current \em the one you need
after a failed unattended refresh.
.br
An archive that cannot be written stops the publication rather than being warned
about. Publishing without one is the only outcome here that is unrecoverable: the
rollback target is destroyed by the very operation that needed it. A lock that is
not there at all is not that case \em it is the first pin.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh\-ranges
Fetches the published ranges and publishes their prefix list. Measures nothing and
spends no bandwidth budget, which is what makes it the mode an installation runs
before the router starts: the response rewriter refuses to CONSTRUCT without a
published prefix list, so
.B systemctl start mosdns\-router
on a machine that has never run it binds nothing on port 53. An installation runs
this with no origin named, so a valid cached range document republishes with no
network at all.
.br
Where nothing is cached either, it publishes the snapshot the package installs at
.IR /usr/share/mosdns\-router/cloudflare\-ranges.json ,
beside the lock that accounts for it \em and only after verifying the snapshot
against that lock. A snapshot whose bytes the lock does not record, a lock that
records no date or no digest of the prefix list, and a snapshot taken from a
different endpoint are all refusals, and nothing is published: a selector over
ranges this project cannot name is worse than an installation that does not
happen. The order is the origin, then this machine's own published document, then
the package's snapshot. A machine that can reach the origin publishes today's
ranges and says
.B "ranges-source: origin" ;
a machine that cannot keeps the ranges its selector was built against and never
re\-pins them; and a machine that has published nothing at all gets the snapshot
the package was built against. The report says which of the three answered, and
how old the snapshot is.
.TP
.B \-\-pinned\-ranges\ \fIPATH\fR
.TQ
.B \-\-pinned\-ranges\-lock\ \fIPATH\fR
Where the two pinned files above are, defaulting to the paths this package
installs them at. The installer runs this command with no paths at all, so the
defaults are the only thing that makes an offline install possible.
.SS test
Measures which Cloudflare address serves this machine best, and reports it. One
invocation of
.B test \-\-apply
is the whole nightly: the measurement phases and the final identity proof in one
run, so the address that is published was proved against what was just measured
rather than against a report written two hours ago. That is why the unit runs this
verb and not
.BR "apply" (1).
.PP
Without
.B \-\-apply
it publishes nothing and changes nothing, and it still spends the bandwidth budget
\(em the measurement is the expensive part and the write is free. A report\-only run
is therefore not a free look at the network.
.PP
Run it by hand as root; the published files are owned by the control identity and
mode 0640, so an unprivileged run cannot write them.
.RB [ \-\-report\ \fIPATH\fR ]
writes the measurement document there as well as to standard output, and is what
makes
.BR apply (1)
possible. It refuses rather than overwriting an existing file, so a report is never
destroyed by re\-running the command that wrote it.
.PP
.SS "What the measurement does"
Seven phases, in this order, and the order is the design rather than an
implementation detail:
.TP
.B collect
Candidates are grouped by provider and hostname. The addresses already in service
are added back as retained candidates, so a measurement cannot forget the address
that is currently working.
.TP
.B tcp
Three connection samples per candidate, giving p50, p95, jitter and loss.
.TP
.B identity
An HTTPS request per identity profile, proving the candidate is the provider it
claims to be and measuring TLS, TTFB and total time. A candidate that fails here
is never scored.
.TP
.B latency
The proved candidates are ranked by latency and the best
.I cdn.latency_candidate_count
(10 by default) go on to the expensive phase.
.TP
.B bandwidth
A bounded download per candidate. This is what the daily budget is spent on.
.TP
.B score
Latency, bandwidth and stability are combined as 0.45, 0.45 and 0.10. Candidates
are drawn from the union of the top few by latency and the top few by bandwidth, so
neither measurement alone decides. A candidate over the loss or latency ceiling is
excluded rather than ranked.
.TP
.B final\-proof
With
.B \-\-apply
only: the winner is proved once more under the control lock, immediately before it
is written. An address that stopped serving between the measurement and the write
is refused here and nothing is published.
.PP
A candidate is not replaced merely by scoring higher. The new address has to beat
the one in service by
.IR cdn.switch_improvement_percent " (10 by default),"
or the current address is kept. A run that cannot find an improvement is a normal
outcome, not a failure.
.SS "The report"
Standard output, one
.I key: value
per line, and every line name below is stable. What a reader usually wants is:
.TP
.B outcome:
the run's own verdict. Per\-group
.B outcome:
lines follow, and the two are not the same answer \(em one group publishing while
another keeps its mapping is "published" overall and "kept" for the group that did
not. A group that kept its address says why in
.BR outcome\-reason: .
.TP
.B applied: \fIADDRESS\fR
What is now in service, or
.B "applied: nothing"
when
.B \-\-apply
was not given.
.TP
.BR budget\-used\-bytes: ", " budget\-limit\-bytes: ", " budget\-remaining\-bytes:
The day's budget. See
.B BANDWIDTH BUDGET
below before running this by hand.
.TP
.BR budget\-exhausted: ", " settle\-refused:
Set when the run could not measure. Every group then keeps its mapping.
.TP
.B final\-proof:
Whether the address about to be published was proved again.
.B "not run"
is the correct line for a report\-only run and does not mean anything failed.
.TP
.BR "  candidate: ", "    detail: ", "  winner: ", "  no\-winner: "
Per\-candidate lines, indented under their
.BR group: .
A candidate that was excluded says which ceiling it was over; one that qualified
carries its score and whether a switch was allowed.
.PP
.BR generated\-at: ", " config\-sha256:
The report's own timestamp and the digest of the configuration it measured. The
configuration digest is what
.B apply
checks, so a report written against one policy cannot be applied to another.
.PP
.BR stale\-candidates: ", " p10\-bytes\-per\-second: ", " identity\-body\-bytes: ", " skipped\-retained: ,
Measurement detail. A stale candidate set means the ranges in use no longer match
what the source publishes today.
.SS apply
Publishes an address from a report written earlier, without measuring again. The
report is at most two hours old, measured from
.BR generated\-at: ,
and was written against the configuration this machine is still running; a report
failing either check is refused and nothing is published.
.PP
This is the verb to reach for when a measurement has already been taken and read,
and the answer is not to change anything now. When the answer is to change something
with fresh evidence, run
.B test \-\-apply
instead \(em it re\-measures and re\-proves in one invocation, so an operator who
wants a current answer is never stuck with an old report. Nothing schedules this
verb; it is an operator's.
.SS "pin, unpin"
.B pin
stops the optimizer for one address, which is how an operator keeps a chosen IP in
service. As root:
.RB " sudo mosdns\-cdnctl pin " 203.0.113.7
proves the address against every identity profile under the control lock before
storing it, so a pin cannot publish an address that does not serve. The selector
goes to
.BR manual .
.PP
Three consequences of that mode, all of them intended and none of them obvious:
.TP
.IP \(bu 2
The nightly run refuses to publish anything and exits
.BR 3 .
The unit's
.B SuccessExitStatus=4
does not excuse 3, so the timer shows a failure. That failure is the pin working.
.TP
.IP \(bu 2
.B unpin
returns the selector to
.BR auto ,
and until the next nightly runs there is
.B no address in service:
the pinned address moves to
.B fallback_ip
and the proof is cleared. Queries use the provider's own answer until then, which
is correct but is not the same as an optimization. This is the one to know about
before unpinning on a machine that was relying on it.
.TP
.IP \(bu 2
The health check can still move a pinned address out of service.
.B health\-check
does not honour the pin: if the pinned address fails
.I cdn.health.failure_threshold
times it is moved to the fallback. A pin is a statement about which address to
prefer, not a claim that the address is immortal.
.PP
A per\-hostname CloudFront mapping in
.I cloudfront\-domains.yaml
is not affected by either verb.
.SS "Turning the optimization off"
Three ways, and they are not equivalent.
.TP
.B "Stop scheduling it"
.B sudo systemctl disable \-\-now mosdns\-cdn\-optimizer.timer
Nothing runs nightly. Whatever address is published stays published until its
proof window closes \(em see below.
.TP
.B "Stop it and use one address"
.BR pin ,
then
.BR "systemctl disable \-\-now mosdns\-cdn\-optimizer.timer" .
This is the setting for an operator who has a preferred address and does not want
anything else considered.
.TP
.B "Stop rewriting entirely"
Set
.B "mode: disabled"
in
.IR /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/cdn\-selector.json .
The rewriter then returns the provider's own address for every query, the health
check does nothing, and
.B test \-\-apply
and
.B pin
both refuse against a disabled selector.
.PP
Nothing in this package writes that mode. It is reachable only by hand, deliberately:
there is no flag for it, because a switch that silently stops an optimization
nobody asked to stop is a worse default than no switch. Edit the file while the
router is running; the rewriter reads it about twice a second.
.SS status
Prints published state as sorted
.I key=value
lines, and writes nothing. It is the supported way to see what the router is
actually using, rather than what a report said at some earlier moment.
.PP
At least one path is required. The three state documents are independent and may
be asked for together, in which case each is prefixed with its name.
.TP
.B \-\-selector \fIPATH\fR
The CDN selector. On a default installation:
.RB " sudo mosdns\-cdnctl status \-\-selector /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/cdn\-selector.json"
prints
.BR generation: ", " mode: ", " provider: ", " schema_version: ",\nand then
.BR winner_ip: ", " fallback_ip: ", " winner_proof_until: " and " last_success:
when they are set. An empty optional field is omitted rather than printed blank, so
a missing winner cannot be mistaken for a configured one.
.B winner_proof_until
in the past means the address is published but
.B not being used
\(em see
.BR "The proof window" .
.PP
.B "mode: auto, manual or disabled"
is the whole of the selector's state, and a fourth value is refused rather than
interpreted.
.TP
.B \-\-dhcp \fIPATH\fR
The DHCP bridge's published upstream set \(em which resolvers the domestic branch is
forwarding to, which interface they came from, and which source answered.
.IR /run/mosdns/dhcp\-upstreams.json .
.TP
.B \-\-ech \fIPATH\fR
The forced\-ECH state \(em which source the key came from, its
.BR public_name: ", its digests and its expiry.
.IR /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/ech\-state.json .
.PP
A missing or invalid document is a named refusal on standard error and exit 3; a
missing file is the normal answer on a machine where that feature has never run.
Values containing a control character are refused whole rather than printed.
.SS "The proof window"
The rewriter only substitutes an address while
.B winner_proof_until
is in the future, and the window is five minutes. It is refreshed by
.B health\-check
on every passing run, which is why that timer runs every two minutes.
.PP
This has a consequence worth stating plainly: if
.BR mosdns\-cdn\-health.timer (5)
is stopped, or the health check keeps failing,
.B the rewrite stops about five minutes later and nothing anywhere says so.
DNS keeps working \(em queries get the provider's own address \(em but the
optimization is gone, silently. If the address in service appears to have stopped
being used, check that timer first:
.BR "systemctl status mosdns\-cdn\-health.timer" .
.SS "BANDWIDTH BUDGET"
The daily budget is what makes a nightly measurement affordable, and it is
.I cdn.bandwidth.daily_budget
(104857600 by default, which is
.I per_candidate_limit
times ten). Each candidate's measurement is bounded by
.I per_candidate_limit
and
.IR per_candidate_seconds .
.PP
The shipped numbers mean one full run of
.B test
spends the entire day's budget:
.I latency_candidate_count
is 10 and
.I per_candidate_limit
is one tenth of the day. So the first
.B test
an operator runs by hand is the last measurement that day, and the next nightly
find
.B budget-exhausted: true
\(em which is
.B not
a failure. It exits 0, and every group keeps the mapping it already had, because a
run that cannot afford to measure does not get to change the address in service.
.PP
Read
.B budget\-remaining\-bytes:
in the report before running this by hand on a machine you care about. Waiting for
the 03:00 timer, or raising
.I daily_budget
in
.IR policy.yaml ,
both work; what does not work is expecting a hand run to be free.
.SS "The foreign route"
.PP
Foreign queries leave this machine through a
.B list
of upstreams named in
.BR policy.yaml (5),
not through one address baked into a program. Each entry is either the resolver
this package installs or an address mosdns dials itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
foreign:
  concurrent: 2
  upstreams:
    - kind: dnscrypt
      name: quad9\-dnscrypt
    - kind: upstream
      name: quad9\-doq
      addr: quic://dns.quad9.net:853
      bootstrap:
        - 9.9.9.9:53
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B kind
says what the entry is.
.B dnscrypt
means the packaged resolver, reached on its own loopback listener; such an entry
carries no
.BR addr ,
because that listener is not a configured address and two copies of it in two
files is how the two documents come to disagree about where the resolver is.
.B upstream
means an address mosdns dials, in any scheme mosdns accepts.
.B name
identifies the entry in every report, refusal and log line, and must be unique.
.B enabled
defaults to true; set it false to keep an entry without using it.
.B addr
and
.B bootstrap
are described below.
.PP
.SS "concurrent: 1 is a random pick, not a race"
.PP
.B concurrent
is how many upstreams
.I one query
is sent to. It defaults to
.BR 2 .
.PP
At
.B 1
mosdns picks ONE upstream at random. That is load balancing, not redundancy: a
reader who has configured four upstreams and set
.B concurrent: 1
has four routes and no failover, and nothing in the document will say so. At
.BR 2 ,
one query is sent to two upstreams and the first answer is returned, so one
unreachable upstream does not stop the query \(em which is what the shipped
.B dnscrypt
entry is there for, since a resolver outage would otherwise be a total loss of
foreign resolution.
.PP
The cost of 2 is one extra outbound attempt per query and one log line per
failed attempt. It is
.I not
latency: each attempt carries its own timeout and the query returns on the first
answer that arrives, so a slow upstream cannot delay a fast one.
.PP
.BR concurrent: 3
is the largest value mosdns honours and
.B 1
is the only value that changes what the setting
.I means
rather than how much work is done.
.PP
.SS "bootstrap is not a query path"
.PP
.B bootstrap
names resolvers that resolve
.I this entry's own host name
and nothing else. It is not a list of resolvers foreign queries are sent to, and
reading
.B policy.yaml
alone cannot tell the difference \(em a domain upstream carrying a bootstrap looks,
in that file, exactly like a route through those resolvers. It is there because
this machine's only resolver is the router itself, so an entry naming a domain
cannot be dialled until something else resolves that domain.
.PP
At most one bootstrap per entry. mosdns parses the whole value as a single
address, so two would render into a configuration the router refuses to load. Give
each resolver its own entry; the same
.B addr
with a different
.B bootstrap
answers the same queries from the same place.
.PP
.SS "The ECH key is fetched through one of them"
.PP
The key a force\-ECH domain needs is fetched through a separate address from the
one queries use, and the policy must leave at least one enabled entry that can
carry it. The rule is measured rather than stylistic: mosdns's UDP upstream re\-sends
a query that has gone unanswered and can drop an answer that arrived early, and an
ECH fetch is the one exchange on this path with no client watching it. So the key
is fetched over TCP only, and a
.B dnscrypt
entry is preferred over any other because the packaged resolver is already running
and already proven at install time \(em an ECH failure is diagnosable against it.
.PP
A policy left with no such entry is
.B refused by name
rather than rendered. The consequence of ignoring that refusal is that every
force\-ECH domain fails closed once the key's grace period ends, with nothing in
the log saying why. A
.B udp://
entry is still routable and is never chosen for this.
.PP
.SS "Disabling the dnscrypt entry does not stop the resolver"
.PP
Setting
.B enabled: false
on the
.B dnscrypt
entry stops
.I this router
from sending queries there. It does not stop
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy.service ,
which keeps running and keeps binding its listener, and the installer still
requires that listener to answer before it reports success. An operator who expects
the unit to be gone will find it running; stopping it is a separate decision made
with
.BR systemctl .
.PP
.SS "Resolvers this package ships"
.PP
The DNSCrypt stamps stay in
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy.toml (5),
which is a conffile, so adding a DNSCrypt provider is a hand edit there followed
by a restart \(em it is deliberately not in
.BR policy.yaml ,
because a provider is a trust decision rather than a routing one. The committed
policy names at most one upstream per provider, so the rendered routing document is
reproducible from
.B policy.yaml
alone; a policy naming a resolver outside the published set is refused by the
package's own gate.
.PP
.SS check\-upstream
.PP
Reports whether each enabled entry of the foreign route can be dialled and can
answer, one line per entry, in policy order, whether it answered or not.
.PP
It exists because "is my DNS working" has two very different answers \(em the
router is fine and one upstream is unreachable, or every upstream is unreachable
\(em and from the router's behaviour they look identical: foreign names simply do
not resolve. This verb asks each entry directly and says which one.
.PP
Every entry is asked about
.BR example.com ,
IANA's reserved example domain, which every recursive resolver is required to
answer. That is a decision rather than a default: a failure is therefore the
transport or the reachability and not one provider's filtering, which asking a
named provider's own domain would have mixed in.
.PP
The
.B dnscrypt
entry is checked over TCP against the packaged listener, which is the same road
the ECH key is fetched through \(em so that line also answers "can this machine
still fetch an ECH key".
.PP
A resolver that
.I answers
but refuses is reported with the rcode it answered and is not the same finding as
one that could not be reached: the first needs a policy and the second needs a
route.
.PP
Exit status is reachability and nothing else: 0 when every enabled entry answered,
3 when at least one did not. It writes nothing and takes no lock.
.PP
.SS flush\-cache
.PP
Empties the router's foreign answer cache, through the cache plugin's own endpoint.
It is not a second mechanism this project invented: there is no second flush path
that could disagree with the router's.
.PP
It writes nothing, takes no lock and restarts nothing \(em an operator clearing a
cache is not refused because a measurement happened to be publishing. When the
router refuses, the router's own words are reported, because a router that is not
listening on its API address and a router that is down are different faults.
.PP
.SS health\-check
Takes the address in service out of service if it is failing. It proves the
published winner and every published per\-hostname mapping against their own
identity profiles, records the verdict, and moves the selector to the fallback
when the winner has failed the policy's threshold. It spends no bandwidth budget
and cannot: the prober it is given offers no transfer. Scheduled every two minutes
by
.BR mosdns\-cdn\-health.timer (5).
.SS emergency\-rollback
ROOT ONLY, and the one verb here that changes a machine's DNS. It restores only
the last valid backup the installer recorded, verifies through the system resolver
that the machine can resolve, and leaves the binaries and the configuration
installed for diagnosis. It does not consult the preflight, so it works on a
machine whose preflight refuses \(em which is the machine whose install died. It
runs the installed installer with an argument array and no shell, so nothing an
operator controls can be reinterpreted on the way there.
.SS emergency\-rollback, unattended
.BR mosdns\-watchdog.service (5)
runs exactly this verb, unattended, once a window of failures has been reached,
and reports the status this verb returned. Nothing here is a second rollback
path: the argument array is the one an operator types, and the watchdog cannot
take a route a human would not.

The watchdog's default is ON, and
.I /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml
is where it is switched off. What a successful rollback restores and what it
does not is stated in
.BR mosdns\-router (8)
and in that file's own comments: basic connectivity comes back and
foreign\-name resolution through DNSCrypt does not, so the fault this package
was installed to fix is no longer in the way and is not fixed.
.SH SHARED STATE
The optimizer, the health check and an operator's own command share one control
lock and one set of state files under
.IR /var/lib/mosdns/runtime .
Whichever process wins the race creates a file at mode 0640 inside a group\-writable
setgid directory, so the other identity can read it, rename over it and take the
lock without any additional privilege. "The other one got there first" is exit
status 4, and it is a different answer from a failure: the two timer units excuse
4 and fail on everything else.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
The policy. Edit this.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/mosdns.yaml
The generated routing document.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/dnscrypt\-proxy.toml
The generated resolver document. Custom stamps are edited HERE, and they need a
dnscrypt\-proxy restart to take effect.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml
Whether the watchdog performs
.B emergency\-rollback
unattended, and the consecutive\-failure count and elapsed\-time window it waits
for. Ships with
.BR "automatic: true" .
.TP
.I /run/mosdns/watchdog/watchdog.json
The watchdog's failure streak, mode 0600, in a directory of its own at 0700
.BR root:root .
Both are deliberate:
.BR /run/mosdns " itself is"
.BR "root:mosdns\-router 2770 with a default ACL, because the DHCP bridge publishes"
there as
.BR mosdns\-router\-cdn ,
and unlink and rename are the containing directory's decision rather than a
file's, so a 0600 file in that directory could still be deleted or replaced by
that identity. Nothing but the watchdog reads this record.
.PP
It is on a tmpfs and does not survive a reboot, deliberately: the first minutes
after a boot are when the router is still coming up, and a machine that has just
been rebooted gets its window again. That tmpfs is the guard in force, and it is
the real one.
.PP
The record also names the boot it belongs to, which is a second answer to the
same question for a machine whose
.B /run
is a disk rather than a tmpfs \-\- and it does **not** fire in this package's own
unit.
.BR mosdns\-watchdog.service (5)
carries the packaged
.BR ProcSubset=pid ,
which mounts a reduced
.B /proc
with no
.BR /proc/sys ,
so the kernel's
.I boot_id
cannot be read inside it and the record carries no boot id. Each run says so.
Run the verb by hand, where
.B /proc/sys
is readable, and the boot id is recorded and a carried\-over streak re\-seeded.
An absent record never means "healthy"; it means nothing has failed yet. The
elapsed half of the window is measured on the machine's boot clock, so a wall
clock that steps cannot satisfy it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/control.lock
The shared control lock, mode 0640, created on first acquire. It is also taken,
for the window in which a machine's DNS is being moved, by the install
transaction and by its rollback, and read by the resolver watchdog before it
counts or acts: while anything else is changing this machine, a failure of
.B 127.0.0.1:53
is the change rather than a fault in it. The watchdog opens it read-only and
treats its absence as free, because every holder creates it first; the
transaction and the rollback create it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/lists/cn\-domains.txt
The pinned China domain list, published by
.B update\-lists
and verified against
.IR source\-lock.json ,
mode 0640. Both are the operator's rather than the package's: an upgrade never
replaces them, because replacing a list somebody reviewed is how a reviewed pin
becomes one nobody reviewed.
.I source\-lock.json
records the digest of the list beside it, so the pair is self\-describing; a list
whose digest does not match its lock is refused at install time rather than
classified against.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/cdn\-selector.json
The published address in service, mode 0640, and the file the whole optimization
exists to write. Read it with
.B status \-\-selector
rather than by eye; the fields are
.BR generation: ", " mode: ", " provider: ", " schema_version: ", " winner_ip: ", "
.BR fallback_ip: ", " winner_proof_until: " and " last_success: ,
and the two timestamps are RFC 3339 in UTC.
.PP
It is a conffile's worth of state in a directory dpkg does not own, and it is
written by the optimizer, the health check and an operator's own
.BR pin ,
so
.B generation
increases monotonically and a reader that sees it go backwards is looking at a
document that was replaced rather than updated. A selector naming a winner with no
.B last_success
is refused by the router, which is what stops a stale file being treated as a live
one.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/health.json
The health check's verdict on the address in service and on every published
per\-hostname mapping, at mode 0640. Its consecutive\-failure count is what
.I cdn.health.failure_threshold
is compared against before the winner and the fallback are swapped. Read
.B health\-check 's own output rather than this file: it prints the same facts
along with the reason a proof was refused.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/bandwidth\-budget.json
What the day's budget has been spent on, with its lock beside it. This is why a
second
.B test
on the same day measures no bandwidth \(em see
.BR "BANDWIDTH BUDGET" .
Delete it to reset the day, which is occasionally useful on a router being
diagnosed and is not something to do routinely.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/lists/cloudflare\-prefixes.txt
The prefix list the response rewriter classifies against, mode 0644. It is a hard
start requirement: the plugin refuses to CONSTRUCT without it, so a router with no
published ranges binds nothing on port 53.
.B \-\-refresh\-ranges
publishes it, beside
.IR /var/lib/mosdns/lists/cloudflare\-ips.json ,
the document it was rendered from. Both are this machine's own state: the
refresh reads them before it reads the package's snapshot, and nothing in an
install or an upgrade replaces them.
.TP
.I /usr/share/mosdns\-router/cloudflare\-ranges.json
The pinned range document this package ships, with its lock at
.IR /usr/share/mosdns\-router/cloudflare\-ranges.lock.json .
The lock records the endpoint it came from, the revision, when it was taken, and
the digests of both the snapshot and the prefix list it renders to; the install
verifies the snapshot's digest before it runs any transaction and refuses
without touching the machine's DNS if it does not match. It is never re\-pinned:
the published artifacts above are written only by
.BR \-\-refresh\-ranges ,
which reads them first.
.PP
Neither of the two is a conffile, so
.BR dpkg (1)
replaces both with the new package's on every upgrade
.BR without asking .
That is deliberate -- they are the ranges a selector classifies every query
against, and two files nobody edited that differ from the package are two files
this package can no longer account for, since the install verifies the snapshot
against a lock the same upgrade replaced. The cost is the one an operator
re\-pinning by hand has to know: such a pin survives exactly until the next
upgrade, and the machine is then back on the ranges that package was built with,
with nothing having said so. Reinstall from wherever you install it, rather than
editing the pair in place.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The verb did what was asked.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, or a report the command wrote to standard output.
.TP
.B 3
The command produced no report at all, or refused to publish. It is also what a
pinned selector produces when a measurement tries to apply, which is the pin
working rather than a fault.
.TP
.B 4
The shared control lock was held by another process, or a state could not be
published.
.PP
The resolver watchdog has its own statuses and they are not this verb's. It
exits 0 when the action finished or when there was nothing to do, its own 1 when
it declined, and otherwise the action's own status passed through unchanged.
Its 1 includes the control lock being held, in which case no failure was
counted and no action taken.
